scDifformer: diffusion-based post-training for virtual cell modeling across large-scale single-cell data
Results
Cross-modal application of scDifformer to spatial spot deconvolution
To extend the application of scDifformer embeddings beyond conventional transcriptomic analyses, we investigated their utility in spatial transcriptomics deconvolution, a task that aims to infer the underlying cell-type composition of spatial spots. Spatial deconvolution offers a potential avenue for bridging single-cell and spatial transcriptomic modalities by leveraging the cell-type information learned from scRNA-seq to interpret spatial data. Given the performance of scDifformer embeddings in cell annotation, we hypothesized that they could also enhance spatial deconvolution accuracy.
We integrated scDifformer-derived embeddings with a graph neural network, GraphSAGE [
[46](#B46)
], creating a hybrid GraphSAGE-scDifformer model (Fig.
[5A](#F5)
; see "Materials and methods" section). To benchmark its performance, we generated simulated spatial transcriptomics (ST) data by gridding cell coordinates from a spatially resolved single-cell dataset [
[43](#B43)
], resulting in 253 artificial spots with known cell type compositions (Fig.
[5B](#F5)
). We compared our model against seven established deconvolution methods spanning multiple algorithmic categories: probabilistic (RCTD [
[86](#B86)
], STRIDE [
[87](#B87)
], Cell2location [
[88](#B88)
]), deep learning (Tangram [
[89](#B89)
]), non-negative matrix factorization (SpatialDWLS [
[90](#B90)
]), graph-based approaches (DSTG [
[91](#B91)
], stdGCN [
[45](#B45)
]), and a spatial transcriptomics foundation model-based approach (scGPT-spatial [
[92](#B92)
]).

